course: dictionary
lesson: 1289
title: Buttress: a word that holds things up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A buttress is a thick wall or support built against another wall to hold it up. Read the context: «Rainforest soil is thin, so roots stay near the surface» tells you the trees need extra help to stand. The clue «prop them up» means hold up, so a buttress is a support.
explain: Buttress can also be a verb: to buttress something is to support or strengthen it. «Buttress your argument with evidence» means add evidence to make your argument stronger. Check by swapping in a simpler word: "support your argument with evidence" still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
explain: Buttress roots are wide, flat roots that spread out like walls around a tall rainforest tree. A flying buttress is an arch that reaches from a separate pillar to support a tall wall. The idea is the same: something outside helps hold up the main structure.
fact: A buttress is a thick wall or support of stone or brick that holds up another wall.
fact: «Buttress roots spread out like walls around many rainforest trees and hold the tall tree up in thin soil.»
fact: A flying buttress is an arch that reaches from a separate pillar to support a tall wall.
fact: Medieval builders used flying buttresses so cathedrals could have tall, thin walls and big stained-glass windows.
fact: To buttress an idea means to support or strengthen it, as in «buttress your argument with evidence».
exercise: «Buttress roots spread out like walls around many rainforest trees.» What is a buttress root? | *A wide, flat root that holds up a tall tree | A type of heavy rain | A small green leaf on the forest floor | A river that floods the forest
exercise: «Buttress your argument with evidence.» What does buttress mean here? | Shout loudly | Throw away | *Support or strengthen
exercise: Medieval builders used flying buttresses so cathedral walls could be tall and thin. What is a flying buttress? | A bell that rings in the tower | A very small window at the top of a tower | *An arch that reaches from a separate pillar to support a tall wall | A wall painted in bright colours
test: Which sentence uses buttress as a verb? | *Mia buttressed her story with facts from a book | The roots of the tree were wide and flat | The cathedral had a tall buttress beside its wall | The buttress was built of stone
test: What does a flying buttress reach from? | The roof of a tent | *A separate pillar | A tree trunk in the rainforest | A river bank
exam: Why does a rainforest tree grow buttress roots? | To catch falling leaves | To store water for the dry months | To make the tree grow faster in the sun | *To hold the tall tree up in thin soil
